{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FMd6Yh2wG4TpHmX5KVB6Ub","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Build — 2026-10-07\n2\t\n3\t## Template: @one-a-day/sports/chess-game-recap/v1\n4\t\n5\t**Variant a** — Clear, readable layout with opening name header, result label (WIN/LOSS/DRAW) in large text on left, time control and rating change stacked on right, date and opponent in footer · gate: clean · render: ok · stills: all elements visible and properly sized at landscape, portrait, and square\n6\t\n7\t## Layout\n8\t\n9\tThe template renders:\n10\t- Header (top 18%): Opening name centered and large\n11\t- Main zone (center 60%): Left side shows result label in large readable type, right side shows time control above rating change\n12\t- Footer (bottom 22%): Date left-aligned, opponent name (with \"vs\" prefix) right-aligned\n13\t\n14\tAll text is readable at small (640×360) and large (3840×2160) canvases. No clipping, proper contrast on dark background.\n15\t\n16\t## Why-tutorial\n17\t\n18\t**Problem page**: Chess players screenshot games to share, but screenshots scale poorly and lose metadata. Streamers need clean, shareable recap cards that render at any size.\n19\t\n20\t**Solution page**: A chess recap card rendering opening, result, time control, rating delta, date and opponent with SVG rendering for vector-sharp scaling at any size.\n21\t\n22\t## What was hard\n23\t\n24\tThe layout contract required precision at seven different canvas sizes (640×360 through 3840×2160). Quantization errors at small canvases meant the constraint margin needed to be tighter than initially declared. Text fitting across such a wide range requires careful padding (cell × 0.94 - 2px rule) to absorb rounding errors. The binding contract demanded all user-controllable props be bound to their source rects (opening, timeControl, date, opponentName).\n25\t\n26\t## In place now: a\n27\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ea02c46e-c14d-4eb6-a719-0c805